</div></p>

<p>Learn how to use Chrome and DevTools to find memory issues that affect page performance, including
memory leaks, memory bloat, and frequent garbage collections.</p>

<h2 id="summary" data-text="Summary" tabindex="-1">Summary</h2>

<ul>
<li>Find out how much memory your page is using with the Chrome Task Manager.</li>
<li>Visualize memory usage over time with Timeline recordings.</li>
<li>Identify detached DOM trees (a common cause of memory leaks) with Heap Snapshots.</li>
<li>Find out when new memory is being allocated in your JS heap with Allocation Timeline recordings.</li>
<li>Identify detached elements retained by JavaScript reference.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="overview" data-text="Overview" tabindex="-1">Overview</h2>

<p>In the spirit of the <a href="https://web.dev/rail">RAIL</a> performance model, the focus of your performance efforts should be
your users.</p>

<p>Memory issues are important because they are often perceivable by users. Users can perceive memory
issues in the following ways:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>A page&#39;s performance gets progressively worse over time.</strong> This is possibly a symptom of a
memory leak. A memory leak is when a bug in the page causes the page to progressively use more and
more memory over time.</li>
<li><strong>A page&#39;s performance is consistently bad.</strong> This is possibly a symptom of memory bloat. Memory
bloat is when a page uses more memory than is necessary for optimal page speed.</li>
<li><strong>A page&#39;s performance is delayed or appears to pause frequently.</strong> This is possibly a symptom of
frequent garbage collections. Garbage collection is when the browser reclaims memory. The browser
decides when this happens. During collections, all script execution is paused. So if the browser
is garbage collecting a lot, script execution is going to get paused a lot.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="memory_bloat_how_much_is_too_much" data-text='Memory bloat: how much is "too much"?' tabindex="-1">Memory bloat: how much is "too much"?</h3>

<p>A memory leak is easy to define. If a site is progressively using more and more memory, then you&#39;ve
got a leak. But memory bloat is a bit harder to pin down. What qualifies as &quot;using too much memory&quot;?</p>

<p>There are no hard numbers here, because different devices and browsers have different capabilities.
The same page that runs smoothly on a high-end smartphone might crash on a low-end smartphone.</p>

<p>The key here is to use the RAIL model and focus on your users. Find out what devices are popular
with your users, and then test out your page on those devices. If the experience is consistently
bad, the page may be exceeding the memory capabilities of those devices.</p>

<h2 id="monitor_memory_use_in_realtime_with_the_chrome_task_manager" data-text="Monitor memory use in realtime with the Chrome Task Manager" tabindex="-1">Monitor memory use in realtime with the Chrome Task Manager</h2>

<p>Use the Chrome Task Manager as a starting point to your memory issue investigation. The Task Manager
is a real-time monitor that tells you how much memory a page is using.</p>

<ol>
<li><p>Press Shift+Esc or go to the Chrome main menu and select <strong>More tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Task manager</strong> to
open the Task Manager.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager.png" alt="Opening the Task Manager." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/opening-task-manager_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p></li>
<li><p>Right-click on the table header of the Task Manager and enable <strong>JavaScript memory</strong>.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory.png" alt="Enabling JS memory in Task manager header." width="770" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/enabling-js-memory_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p></li>
</ol>

<p>These two columns tell you different things about how your page is using memory:</p>

<ul>
<li>The <strong>Memory footprint</strong> column represents OS memory. DOM nodes are stored in OS memory. If this
value is increasing, DOM nodes are getting created.</li>
<li><p>The <strong>JavaScript Memory</strong> column represents the JS heap. This column contains two values. The
value you&#39;re interested in is the live number (the number in parentheses). The live number
represents how much memory the reachable objects on your page are using. If this number is
increasing, either new objects are being created, or the existing objects are growing.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager.png" alt="Task Manager with the Javascript memory header enabled." width="770" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/task-manager_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p></li>
</ul>

<h2 id="visualize_memory_leaks_with_timeline_recordings" data-text="Visualize memory leaks with Performance recordings" tabindex="-1">Visualize memory leaks with Performance recordings</h2>

<p>You can also use the <strong>Performance</strong> panel as another starting point in your investigation. The Performance
panel helps you visualize a page&#39;s memory use over time.</p>

<ol>
<li>Open the <strong>Performance</strong> panel in DevTools.</li>
<li>Enable the <strong>Memory</strong> checkbox.</li>
<li><a href="/docs/devtools/evaluate-performance/reference#record-runtime">Make a recording</a>.</li>
</ol>
<aside class="tip"><strong>Tip:</strong><span> It&#39;s a good practice to start and end your recording with a forced garbage collection. To force garbage collection, click
the <span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">mop</span> <strong>Collect garbage</strong> button while recording.</span></aside>
<p>To demonstrate <strong>Performance</strong> memory recordings, consider the following code:</p>
<div></div><devsite-code><pre class="devsite-click-to-copy" translate="no" dir="ltr" is-upgraded syntax="JavaScript"><code translate="no" dir="ltr"><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">x</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">[];</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-kd">function</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">grow</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">()</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">{</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-k">for</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">0</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> &lt; </span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">10000</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">++</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">)</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">{</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">    </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">body</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">appendChild</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">createElement</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'div'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">));</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">}</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">x</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">push</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-ow">new</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">Array</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">1000000</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">).</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">join</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'x'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">));</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-p">}</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">getElementById</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'grow'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">).</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">addEventListener</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'click'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">,</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">grow</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
</code></pre></devsite-code>
<p>Every time that the button referenced in the code is pressed, ten thousand <code translate="no" dir="ltr">div</code> nodes are appended
to the document body, and a string of one million <code translate="no" dir="ltr">x</code> characters is pushed onto the <code translate="no" dir="ltr">x</code> array.
Running this code produces a Timeline recording like the following screenshot:</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example.png" alt="A simple growth example." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/simple-growth-example_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>First, an explanation of the user interface. The <strong>HEAP</strong> graph in the <strong>Overview</strong> pane (below
<strong>NET</strong>) represents the JS heap. Below the <strong>Overview</strong> pane is the <strong>Counter</strong> pane. Here you can
see memory usage broken down by JS heap (same as <strong>HEAP</strong> graph in the <strong>Overview</strong> pane),
documents, DOM nodes, listeners, and GPU memory. Disabling a checkbox hides it from the graph.</p>

<p>Now, an analysis of the code compared with the screenshot. If you look at the node counter (the
green graph) you can see that it matches up cleanly with the code. The node count increases in
discrete steps. You can presume that each increase in the node count is a call to <code translate="no" dir="ltr">grow()</code>. The JS
heap graph (the blue graph) is not as straightforward. In keeping with best practices, the first dip
is actually a forced garbage collection (achieved by pressing the <strong>collect garbage</strong> button). As
the recording progresses you can see that the JS heap size spikes. This is natural and expected: the
JavaScript code is creating the DOM nodes on every button click and doing a lot of work when it
creates the string of one million characters. The key thing here is the fact that the JS heap ends
higher than it began (the &quot;beginning&quot; here being the point after the forced garbage collection). In
the real world, if you saw this pattern of increasing JS heap size or node size, it would
potentially mean a memory leak.</p>

<h2 id="discover_detached_dom_tree_memory_leaks_with_heap_snapshots" data-text="Discover detached DOM tree memory leaks with Heap Snapshots" tabindex="-1">Discover detached DOM tree memory leaks with Heap Snapshots</h2>

<p>A DOM node can only be garbage collected when there are no references to it from either the page&#39;s
DOM tree or JavaScript code. A node is said to be &quot;detached&quot; when it&#39;s removed from the DOM tree but
some JavaScript still references it. Detached DOM nodes are a common cause of memory leaks. This
section teaches you how to use DevTools&#39; heap profilers to identify detached nodes.</p>

<p>Here&#39;s a simple example of detached DOM nodes.</p>
<div></div><devsite-code><pre class="devsite-click-to-copy" translate="no" dir="ltr" is-upgraded syntax="JavaScript"><code translate="no" dir="ltr"><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">detachedTree</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-kd">function</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">create</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">()</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">{</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">ul</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">createElement</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'ul'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-k">for</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">0</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> &lt; </span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">10</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">i</span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">++</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">)</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">{</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">    </span><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">li</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">createElement</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'li'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">    </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">ul</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">appendChild</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">li</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">}</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">detachedTree</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">ul</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">;</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-p">}</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">getElementById</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'create'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">).</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">addEventListener</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'click'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">,</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">create</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
</code></pre></devsite-code>
<p>Clicking the button referenced in the code creates a <code translate="no" dir="ltr">ul</code> node with ten <code translate="no" dir="ltr">li</code> children. These nodes
are referenced by the code but do not exist in the DOM tree, so they&#39;re detached.</p>

<p>Heap snapshots are one way to identify detached nodes. As the name implies, heap snapshots show you
how memory is distributed among your page&#39;s JS objects and DOM nodes at the point of time of the
snapshot.</p>

<p>To create a snapshot, open DevTools and go to the <strong>Memory</strong> panel, select the <strong>Heap Snapshot</strong> radio button, and then press the <strong>Take snapshot</strong> button.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot.png" alt="Take heap snapshot radio button selected." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/take-heap-snapshot_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>The snapshot may take some time to process and load. Once it&#39;s finished, select it from the lefthand panel (named <strong>Heap snapshots</strong>).</p>

<p>Type <code translate="no" dir="ltr">Detached</code> in the <strong>Class filter</strong> input box to search for detached DOM trees.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes.png" alt="Filtering for detached nodes." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/filtering-detached-nodes_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>Expand the carats to investigate a detached tree.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree.png" alt="Investigating a detached tree." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-detached-tree_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>Click a node to investigate it further. In the <strong>Objects</strong> pane you can see more
information about the code that&#39;s referencing it. For example, in the following screenshot you can see
that the <code translate="no" dir="ltr">detachedTree</code> variable is referencing the node. To fix this particular memory leak, you
would study the code that uses <code translate="no" dir="ltr">detachedTree</code> and ensure that it removes its reference to the node
when it&#39;s no longer needed.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object.png" alt="Investigating a detached node." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/investigating-object_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<h2 id="identify_js_heap_memory_leaks_with_allocation_timelines" data-text="Identify JS heap memory leaks with Allocation Timelines" tabindex="-1">Identify JS heap memory leaks with Allocation Timelines</h2>

<p>The <strong>Allocation Timeline</strong> is another tool that can help you track down memory leaks in your JS heap.</p>

<p>To demonstrate the <strong>Allocation Timeline</strong> consider the following code:</p>
<div></div><devsite-code><pre class="devsite-click-to-copy" translate="no" dir="ltr" is-upgraded syntax="JavaScript"><code translate="no" dir="ltr"><span class="devsite-syntax-kd">var</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">x</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-o">=</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">[];</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-kd">function</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">grow</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">()</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">{</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-w">  </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">x</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">push</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-ow">new</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nb">Array</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-mf">1000000</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">).</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">join</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'x'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">));</span>
<span class="devsite-syntax-p">}</span>

<span class="devsite-syntax-nb">document</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">.</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">getElementById</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'grow'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">).</span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">addEventListener</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">(</span><span class="devsite-syntax-s1">'click'</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">,</span><span class="devsite-syntax-w"> </span><span class="devsite-syntax-nx">grow</span><span class="devsite-syntax-p">);</span>
</code></pre></devsite-code>
<p>Every time that the button referenced in the code is pushed, a string of one million characters is
added to the <code translate="no" dir="ltr">x</code> array.</p>

<p>To record an <strong>Allocation Timeline</strong>, open DevTools, go to the <strong>Memory</strong> panel, select the <strong>Allocations on timeline</strong> radio button, press the <span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">radio_button_checked</span> <strong>Record</strong> button, perform the action that you suspect is causing the memory leak, and then press the <img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/stop-recording-button.svg" width="25px" aria-hidden="true"> <strong>Stop recording</strong> button when you&#39;re done.</p>

<p>As you&#39;re recording, notice if any blue bars show up on the <strong>Allocation Timeline</strong>, like in the following screenshot.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations.png" alt="New allocations in the performance timeline." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/new-allocations_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>Those blue bars represent new memory allocations. Those new memory allocations are your candidates
for memory leaks. You can zoom on a bar to filter the <strong>Constructor</strong> pane to only show objects that
were allocated during the specified timeframe.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline.png" alt="A zoomed allocation timeline." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/zoomed-allocation-timeline_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<p>Expand the object and click on its value to view more details about it in the <strong>Object</strong> pane. For
example, in the screenshot below, by viewing the details of the object that was newly allocated,
you&#39;d be able to see that it was allocated to the <code translate="no" dir="ltr">x</code> variable in the <code translate="no" dir="ltr">Window</code> scope.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details.png" alt="Object details of a string array." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/object-details_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<h2 id="allocation-profile" data-text="Investigate memory allocation by function" tabindex="-1">Investigate memory allocation by function</h2>

<p>Use the <strong>Allocation sampling</strong> profile type in the <strong>Memory</strong> panel to view memory allocation by JavaScript function.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile.png" alt="Allocation sampling profiler in Memory panel." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/record-allocation-profile_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<ol>
<li>Select the <strong>Allocation sampling</strong> radio button. If there is a worker on the page, you
can select that as the profiling target from the <strong>Select JavaScript VM instance</strong> window.</li>
<li>Press the <strong>Start</strong> button.</li>
<li>Perform the actions on the page which you want to investigate.</li>
<li>Press the <strong>Stop</strong> button when you have finished all of your actions.</li>
</ol>

<p>DevTools shows you a breakdown of memory allocation by function. The default view is <strong>Heavy (Bottom Up)</strong>, which displays the functions that allocated the most memory at the top.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile.png" alt="Allocation profile results page." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/allocation-profile_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<h2 id="identify_objects_retained_by_js_reference" data-text="Identify objects retained by JS reference" tabindex="-1">Identify objects retained by JS reference</h2>

<p>The <strong>Detached elements</strong> profile shows you detached elements that persist because they are referenced by JavaScript code.</p>

<p>Record a <strong>Detached elements</strong> profile to view the exact HTML nodes and node count.</p>

<p><img src="/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile.png" alt="Detached elements profile example." width="800" srcset="https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_36.png 36w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_48.png 48w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_72.png 72w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_96.png 96w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_480.png 480w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_720.png 720w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_856.png 856w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_960.png 960w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_1440.png 1440w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_1920.png 1920w,https://developer.chrome.com/static/docs/devtools/memory-problems/image/detached-elements-profile_2880.png 2880w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 856px"></p>

<h2 id="spot_frequent_garbage_collections" data-text="Spot frequent garbage collections" tabindex="-1">Spot frequent garbage collections</h2>

<p>If your page appears to pause frequently, then you may have garbage collection issues.</p>

<p>You can use either the Chrome Task Manager or Timeline memory recordings to spot frequent garbage
collections. In the Task Manager, frequently rising and falling <strong>Memory</strong> or <strong>JavaScript Memory</strong>
values represent frequent garbage collections. In Timeline recordings, frequently rising and falling
JS heap or node count graphs indicate frequent garbage collections.</p>

<p>Once you&#39;ve identified the problem, you can use an Allocation Timeline recording to find out where
memory is being allocated and which functions are causing the allocations.</p>

  

  
</div>

  
    
    
      
